Native release binary builds use scripts/build-native.sh inside an actual
Ubuntu 24.04, Ubuntu 26.04 or Debian 13 builder. Rust dependencies are vendored in
the release source archive; cargo always uses --locked --offline. Build-Depends
are checked without dpkg-buildpackage -d.

Ubuntu 24.04's archive compiler is too old for this application. The private
release builder packages the complete pinned upstream Rust 1.90.0 toolchain
using scripts/package-rust-toolchain.sh. This actual build-only Debian package
provides versioned cargo/rustc dependencies, installs under /opt and is never a
runtime dependency or uploaded to the application APT repository. All remaining
Build-Depends must be installed normally. It is not an official Debian/Ubuntu
archive build method: official archive submission requires their approved Rust
and crate packaging and a separate maintainer review.

The generic release source archive has a versioned linux-app-manager-VERSION
root, vendor/, .cargo/config.toml and source-origin.json. For distro-specific
source package construction, name it linux-app-manager_VERSION.orig.tar.gz;
the Debian packaging format is 3.0 (quilt). Binary release versions add -1~SUITE
so each supported distribution build has a distinct filename and package version.
